 ## Background
-The family originates from the application of differentiable rendering techniques to tomographic reconstruction. Early work focused on adapting standard volume rendering pipelines (like DiffDVR) to inverse problems, demonstrating that absorption-only models could be solved efficiently using differentiable optimizers [S1]. The field has since expanded to include specialized physics-based models for modalities like ultrasound, where simple ray-tracing is insufficient to capture wave phenomena [5511-b-mode-image-reconstruction.md].
+The family originates from the application of differentiable rendering techniques to tomographic reconstruction [G]. Early work focused on adapting standard volume rendering pipelines (like DiffDVR) to inverse problems, demonstrating that absorption-only models could be solved using differentiable optimizers, though at higher computational cost than algebraic methods [S1]. The field has since expanded to include specialized physics-based models for modalities like ultrasound, where simple ray-tracing is insufficient to capture wave phenomena [5511-b-mode-image-reconstruction.md].
 
